// Live-action genre: coastal family drama. Competent-band craft calibration.
// Real but deliberate flaws per band design: the clue pays off early rather than
// at the climax, two midsection scenes share a flat "nothing new" beat, one stock
// reassurance line appears, and the Act 3 reconciliation is thin/short.
// Author: STORYMACHINE benchmark contributor, 2026.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE KEEPERS' COTTAGE - MORNING

Gray light through salt-streaked windows. GRETA, 40s, packs a box of nautical charts and a brass lamp. The lighthouse lamp in the tower above is cold and dark for the first time in a season. Outside, a SHIPPING SCHEDULE is taped to the inside of the window, the last ferry of the year circled in red pen.

GRETA
The last ferry leaves Friday at six. I should have left a week ago. Father would say I'm dawdling.

INT. KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS

Greta's brother CAL, 40s, bearded, reads the same shipping schedule over a mug of tea. A red circle marks Friday at 6 p.m. sharp.

CAL
Friday at six. That's the window. After that the island's cut off for the season and the ferry doesn't run again until spring thaw.

GRETA
Then I have three days to settle father's things and go.

CAL
You've had three years, Greta. You just weren't here.

INT. ATTIC - DAY

Greta opens a sea chest bound with tarred rope. Inside, beneath a wool coat, a LOGBOOK bound in oilcloth. She turns to a page dated years ago, the week of the wreck everyone on the island still talks about.

GRETA
(reading aloud)
"The lamp failed at nine. No record of the ship. Could not raise the mainland." That's father's hand. He logged a failure and then he logged nothing.

She closes the logbook. She has her answer, early and plain, and the energy goes out of the room with it.

INT. KITCHEN - NIGHT

Greta and Cal sit across the table from each other, the logbook between them under the hanging lamp. An old grievance that has waited three years for this kitchen.

GRETA
You stayed on the island after father died. You kept the lamp yourself for two seasons. And you never once called me in the city to say how any of it was.

CAL
And you never came back for the funeral. You sent flowers and a check. We both chose the easier silence, Greta. Don't pretend it was only me.

INT. BOATHOUSE - DAY

Cal works on an outboard motor clamped to a workbench. Greta watches from the dock, hands in her coat. The harbor is empty.

CAL
The fuel line is cracked again. Same as last summer.

GRETA
Same as every summer. You'll patch it and it'll crack again next spring.

CAL
Most things on this island work that way.

INT. PORCH - DAY

They drink coffee side by side, mugs steaming in the cold. Gulls wheel over the breakwater. The conversation finds no new ground.

GRETA
Weather's holding, at least.

CAL
For now. They're calling for fog by Thursday.

GRETA
Then I'll pack in the fog.

INT. KITCHEN - NIGHT

Greta re-reads the logbook entry by the stove, as if a second reading will change the words. She already knows what it says.

GRETA
Father knew the lamp failed that night. He wrote it down.

CAL
He knew. He wrote it down, and then he wrote nothing, and then he never reported it to the mainland. I asked him once. He said some things stay on the island.

INT. MAINLAND FERRY OFFICE - DAY

Greta crosses the gangway to the mainland and shows the logbook to a clerk behind a wire window. The clerk shrugs and flips through a ledger of his own.

CLERK
Nothing in our records about a ship in distress that night. Could've been a fishing boat that turned back before it hit the shoal. Happens more than folks think.

GRETA
Then there's nothing to chase. Thank you for your time.

INT. COTTAGE - FRIDAY EVENING

The mantel clock nears six. Greta's bag is packed by the door. Cal stands at the window watching for the ferry's running lights through the early dark.

CAL
I forgive you for not coming back. Try to forgive me for staying.

GRETA
I'll try, Cal. That's the most I can promise from a dock.

She picks up her bag. The ferry horn sounds across the water, distant, then fades toward the mainland. The lamp in the tower above stays dark.

FADE OUT.
